  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1029 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nStrong ridging will continue through the foreseeable future\nacross the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys, including the Mid-\nSouth. Radiosonde observations from Tuesday evening show dry,\nsubsident profiles with the ridge at 596 dm over central\nTennessee with models supporting the idea that the ridge will\nmaintain this strength through the end of the week. Therefore,\nexpect daily temperatures to approach, if not exceed, 100 F with\nheat indices of upwards of 105 - 110 F through Saturday. Surface\ndewpoints are forecast to be slightly \"drier\" than earlier this\nsummer, around 70 F during peak heating, which will keep heat\nindices below 110 F in most areas. So, a Heat Advisory has been\nissued starting from Wednesday at 11 AM CDT through Saturday\nevening. Additionally, the remnants of Tropical Storm Edouard are\nnot expected to impact the Mid-South with the majority of model\nguidance keeping the weakening surface low to our west before\nbeing filled in by the broader ridging over the southern CONUS.\n\nEnsembles seem to be trending towards the continuation of the\nridging over the Mid-South beyond Saturday, but also begin to \nshow signs of increasing model variability at this point in the \nforecast. Some members attempt to bring weaker (588 dm) heights \nwest of the Tennessee River that could lead to increased afternoon\nshower activity, but this signal is still too weak to warrant any\nexpectation of relief from the heat. So, don't get your hopes up \nabout cooler weather in the near future with a growing consensus \nthat dangerous heat may stick around into early next week.",
